verses.text:
They set the sons of Jerusalem up as a laughingstock
in place of its prostitutes;{c}
everyone who passed by went in under the sun’s eye.

Do not delay, God, to repay them on their heads,
to speak against the dragon’s pride
in a single charge.”{e}

I did not have to wait long before God showed me his arrogance:
pierced through on the mountains of Egypt,
of less account than the least of men on land and sea.{f}

For he had treated others with contempt, in dishonor;
he did not reckon that he was a man,
and did not think of what comes afterward.

Praise God, you who revere the Lord with knowledge,
for the Lord’s mercy rests on those who revere him,
and comes along with his verdict,

Sing a hymn, a new song, to God who is worthy of praise.
Sing and be wide awake at his watch,
for a psalm to God from a good heart is good.{a}

Why do you sit, you profane man, in the council of the devout,
while your heart is far away from the Lord,
angering the God of Israel with your lawless acts?

he is filled with lawlessness in this,
and his eyes are on another house, to destroy it with words that will fill his appetite.
His soul is never satisfied, like Hades, in all these things.{b}

I said, “They are making their ways straight in righteousness.”
I thought over the judgments of God from the creation of heaven and earth;
I declared God righteous in his judgments of all time.

The rulers of the land met him with joy,
and said to him, “Your road is blessed;
come, enter in peace.”

They smoothed the rough roads before his entry;
they opened the gates to Jerusalem;
they crowned her walls.

For you chose the descendants of Abraham above all the nations,
and put your name on us, Lord,
and you will not cease forever.{a}

Put on, Jerusalem, your garments of glory;
prepare your holy robe,
for God has spoken good things for Israel forever and ever.

May the Lord do what he has promised for Israel and Jerusalem;
may the Lord raise Israel up by his glorious name.
The Lord’s mercy is on Israel forever and ever.{b}

Like a fire in a crowd, it sets fire to its own beauty;
its guest, the tongue, fills houses with lies,
cutting down trees of gladness, setting the lawless ablaze.{a}

Their planting is rooted forever;
they will not be uprooted all the days of heaven,
for Israel is the portion and the inheritance of God.

Not so the sinners and the lawless,
who loved the day of sharing in their sin;
their desire is in the smallness of decay.{a}

And they did not remember God,
for human conduct is always plain to him,
and he knows the secret rooms of the heart before they exist.

But those who fear the Lord will be shown mercy in it,
and will live by the generosity of their God;
but sinners will perish forever.{a}

But because of our sins sinners rose up against us;
they attacked us and drove us out.
Those to whom you made no promise took it by force,

See, Lord, and raise up for them their king,
the son of David, at the time you have seen, God,
to rule over Israel your servant.{c}

in wisdom and righteousness to drive out sinners from the inheritance,
to smash the arrogance of the sinner like a potter’s jars;
with an iron rod to break all their strength to pieces,

The Lord himself is his king;
the strong man’s hope is the hope he rests in God,
and he will have mercy on all the nations before him in fear.

He will not grow weak in his days, because of his God,
for God has made him strong in the holy spirit,
and wise in counsel with understanding, with strength and righteousness.

and his hope in the Lord will not fail.
And who can stand against him?
He is mighty in what he does and strong through reverence for God;

May God hasten his mercy to Israel;
may he rescue us from the uncleanness of profane enemies.
The Lord himself is our king forever and ever.

And they have not strayed since the day he made them;
from ancient generations they have not left their ways,
unless God commanded them, through the order given by his servants.
